Date: October 20, 2026
System: Proxmox VE host (voyager) and macOS administration client
Status: SSH access working; effective firewall enforcement still requires verification
Goal
Set up SSH key authentication so I could administer Voyager from my MacBook without relying on password entry for every login.
Setup and troubleshooting
I generated a passphrase-protected ED25519 key pair on macOS and configured SSH to work with ssh-agent and Keychain. My initial connection to the host on port 22 was refused. I checked that the SSH service was listening, then investigated the Proxmox firewall configuration. Temporarily stopping the firewall during testing allowed me to proceed with key setup.

I installed the public key on the host and added an inbound SSH allow rule to the Proxmox cluster firewall configuration:
[RULES]
IN SSH(ACCEPT)
I then restored the firewall service and verified SSH access.
Outcome
I could connect to Voyager using my SSH key from the MacBook. The private key remained on the client and was protected by its passphrase.
Remaining verification
The presence of an allow rule and an active proxmox-firewall service does not by itself prove which rules the host is enforcing. The effective firewall backend, rule scope, and inbound policy still need a dedicated review. Root SSH access and the overall access policy also deserve a separate hardening pass.
Lessons learned
- Test SSH service availability, network reachability, and firewall behavior separately.
- Public-key authentication and network access controls solve different problems.
- Keep a local-console recovery path when changing remote-access settings.
